<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joseph François Dupleix</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1697">1697</a>–<a href="/years/1763">1763</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph François Dupleix was governor-general of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pondicherry">Pondicherry</a> from 1742 to 1754. During the Carnatic wars he intervened in the succession disputes of the Carnatic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>, raising sepoy armies and installing allied rulers, and briefly made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the leading European power in southern India. He was recalled in 1754 after setbacks against the British.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-francois-dupleix.jpg" alt="Joseph François Dupleix" width="320" height="370" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Francois_Dupleix.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dupleix was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landrecies">Landrecies</a> on 23 January 1697, the son of François Dupleix, a wealthy fermier général who wanted him to become a merchant and, to turn him from his interest in science, sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1715 on a ship of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-east-india-company">French East India Company</a>. After several voyages to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and India he was named in 1720 to the superior council at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, where besides his official duties he traded on his own account and made a fortune. In 1730 he became superintendent of French affairs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandannagar">Chandernagore</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1741 he married Jeanne Albert, widow of a company councillor, who was known as Joanna Begum and helped him in his dealings with Indian princes. As governor general from 1742 he saw in their succession disputes a chance to advance French interests, adopted a style of oriental splendour and trained Indian troops, called sepoys, as infantry. His bitter rivalry with Bertrand François Mahé de La Bourdonnais, governor of the Isle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>, partly spared the British.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> capitulated to the French in 1746, Dupleix refused to return it to British rule, breaking the treaty La Bourdonnais had signed, and he sent expeditions against Fort St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>. After the peace of Aix-la-Chapelle he sent large forces to support claimants to the Carnatic and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, while the British backed their rivals; in 1750 the Subadar of the Deccan gave the French the Alamparai Fort. From 1751 his envoy Sieur de Bruno built close ties with the Mon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, which came to nothing once the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> drew French attention elsewhere.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The French government, anxious for peace, sent a commissioner in 1754 with orders to supersede and if necessary arrest him, and Dupleix had to embark for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on 12 October 1754. He had spent his private fortune on his policy, but the company refused to repay him and the government regarded him as an ambitious adventurer. He died in obscurity on 10 November 1763. French recognition came in 1870 with two statues, one of them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pondicherry">Puducherry</a>, and streets, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> metro station and four French warships bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joseph François Dupleix” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Joseph-Francois Dupleix</b>, governor from 1742, intervened in the succession disputes of the Carnatic Sultanate and Hyderabad, raising Indian troops and briefly dominating the Deccan, until Robert Clive's victories reversed French fortunes.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-india">French India</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landrecies">Landrecies</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.13,3.69&amp;year=1739" aria-label="Landrecies on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4634 421 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Joseph François Dupleix"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5084 691h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5084 691h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.13,3.69&amp;year=1739">What was here in 1739? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
